#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""homeroom - Hyperspace Homeroom, a tile-based teen comedy of aliens, prom
week and giant rubber monsters, in Python + Tk.

Standard library only.  The engine is the one Miskatonic and Penultimate
share (one hero, a map of squares, people to talk to by keyword, shops,
places underneath), and the rules are those of Teenagers From Outer Space:
eight Stats rolled on one die, a die plus a Stat (and a Knack) against a die
plus a difficulty, silly alien powers, gadgets, and nobody ever getting
hurt, only Bonked.

Marigold Bay, California, the Monday before the Spring Formal.  Marigold Bay
High has been chosen to host the Galactic Spring Formal on Saturday night,
and if the Galaxy's party judges like it, Earth gets listed as a Fun Planet.
If they don't, it gets listed as a rest stop.  The Hyperdisco, the mirror
ball that makes a dance heard across four hundred light-years, has been
smashed into four Disco Shards and hidden around town.  Find them, bring
them to Dr. Fizzwick in the science lab, find a date if you can, and get the
Hyperdisco spinning in the gym before midnight on Saturday.  Someone in
town, one of the grown-ups, is secretly an agent of the planet Snorr, where
fun has been illegal since 1742.

Every new game makes a new week: the shards lie in four of six places (the
school boiler room, the mall's sub-basement, the storm drains, a crashed
saucer up at Lover's Point, a broken arcade machine and the drive-in's
projection booth), the people of Marigold Bay change, and one of them is the
agent.  The town stays where it is.  The game is saved to ~/.homeroom.json
whenever you change place and when you quit, and resumed next time.

Teeners: be a Human, or an alien (Near Human, Not Very Near Human or Real
Weirdie), and pick a clique.  Stats (Smarts, Bod, Relationship With Parents,
Luck, Driving, Looks, Cool and Bonk) are rolled on a die each and can be
moved about, never above 6 or below 1.  Knacks add to a Stat when they fit.
Aliens roll three powers from five tables; Humans roll on the Humans Only
table and can all Fake Out an alien.  To do something hard, roll a die plus
the Stat (plus a Knack) against a die plus a difficulty from 1 to 10; ties
go to the other side.

Bonk: nobody gets hurt.  Every hit costs Bonk; at zero you stand about
looking stupid for a turn, plus a turn for every point below zero, and then
your Bonk comes back all at once.  Get Bonked out three times in one place
and you wake up in the school nurse's office, an hour later and slightly
singed.  Relationship With Parents (RWP) goes down when you're out after
curfew, cut class or wreck things, and comes back with good behaviour; below
zero you're grounded.  Teener Points, earned by doing great things, buy
Knacks or cash.

Talking: walk into a person (or into a counter with someone behind it),
then choose or type a word.  Everyone knows NAME, JOB and BYE; most people
know NEWS, and words in CAPITALS are worth asking about.  Shopkeepers
answer BUY.  Ask someone to the FORMAL, if you dare.

Touch and mouse: tap a square to walk there, tap to aim, tap choices in
menus.  Gamepad (Linux, the Steam Deck): d-pad or stick moves, A waits or
chooses, B backs out, X zaps, Y uses a power, LB uses a gadget, RB the
journal, View the map and Start the menu.

Sound effects and music are synthesized and played with afplay (macOS),
paplay or aplay if one is available, on Android with pyjnius, or with
pygame's mixer; otherwise the game is silent.  Music is cached in
~/.cache/homeroom.

App bundle (macOS):  homeroom --build-app [DIR]   builds homeroom.app
                     homeroom --icon-png SIZE [OUT.png]

Keys: arrows or WASD move, Space waits, E searches, F fires what you hold,
C uses a power, U uses a gadget or a snack, G gets in (or out of) your
wheels, I shows your Teener, J the journal, M the map, ? help.

The tiles are drawn by the code below (PERSON_SHAPES, ART and
draw_terrain): no image files.  homeroom --selftest builds every map, room,
level and tune of a few weeks without opening a window.
"""

